\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A finite non-Gaussian connection mixture}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{The finite logarithm in \ref{fdip-00J7} can contain a nonzero term of degree #{4}. For this example assume all periods #{N_k\geq2} and choose a direction #{j} with #{N_j\geq3}. Take the allowed boundary #{c_W=0} and fix #{x=y+e_j}. These hypotheses exclude loop hops and keep the two neighbors in direction #{j} distinct. Choose an orthonormal spin basis diagonalizing the Hermitian matrix #{\gamma_j} and choose a unit eigenvector with eigenvalue #{\sigma\in\{1,-1\}}. Use that spin component at both sites, with component indices denoted #{i=(x,\alpha)} and #{k=(y,\alpha)}. Put #{G_U=K_U^{-1}} and #{R=d/a}. For #{m>R}, uniformly over all links,
##{G_{ik}=\frac{\sigma h_\gamma}{2am^2}+O(m^{-3}),\qquad
G_{ki}=-\frac{\sigma h_\gamma^{-1}}{2am^2}+O(m^{-3}),\qquad
G_{ii}=m^{-1}+O(m^{-3}),\quad G_{kk}=m^{-1}+O(m^{-3}).}}

\p{The constants depend only on #{a,d} for #{m\geq2R}. These estimates follow from the preceding Neumann remainder with #{\mu=m}: the diagonal of #{V_U} is zero and its two indicated off-diagonal entries are opposite signed spin eigenvalues times the link phase. The positive connection law may itself depend on #{m}; the estimates are uniform in #{U} and so remain valid under that changing probability law.}

\p{The paired four-point formula, obtained by taking 4 source derivatives in \ref{fdip-00IV}, is
##{\langle\psi_i\bar\psi_i\psi_k\bar\psi_k\rangle_U=G_{ii}G_{kk}-G_{ik}G_{ki}.}}

\p{Both averaged off-diagonal entries vanish by gauge invariance. The covariance of the two diagonal entries is #{O(m^{-6})}, since subtracting their common deterministic leading term leaves uniformly #{O(m^{-3})} quantities. However
##{\mathbb E_\nu(G_{ik}G_{ki})=-\frac1{4a^2m^4}+O(m^{-5}).}}

\p{Hence
##{\langle\psi_i\bar\psi_i\psi_k\bar\psi_k\rangle
-\langle\psi_i\bar\psi_i\rangle\langle\psi_k\bar\psi_k\rangle
=\frac1{4a^2m^4}+O(m^{-5})\ne0}}

\p{for all sufficiently large #{m}. A single quadratic Grassmann law with these same two-point functions would give zero on the left by its paired Wick formula and the vanishing off-diagonal entries. Purely barred or purely unbarred two-point functions also vanish, by total charge conservation, so adding such a quadratic pairing cannot repair the mismatch. Thus gauge averaging really can generate higher source correlations. This example concerns the permitted #{c_W=0} boundary and leaves the determinant and covariance statements for all #{c_W\geq0} unchanged.}
